From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wize-logic <wizelogic@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: --runtime-header picks what --split writes as
 recomp_types.h

translate_batch_split always copies templates/runtime/recomp_types.h into
the output directory, refreshed on every run so the header cannot drift
from the lifter (the comment there explains why). A project that needs
its own header was told to "put one earlier on the include path", but
that cannot work: the chunks include recomp_funcs.h, which includes
"recomp_types.h", and a quoted include searches the including file's own
directory before any -I path. The copy in gen/ always wins, and the only
way round it is editing generated output after every run.

A runtime with a different register model needs exactly that. On a
big-endian host guest memory still holds the x86's little-endian bytes,
so its MEM* macros byte-swap, its SIMD unions carry a little-endian
storage order and its atomics swap around the compare-and-swap; it keeps
the same contract with the lifter, just a different implementation.

--runtime-header (translate_batch_split's runtime_header) takes
'template' (the default, unchanged behaviour), a path (copy that file
instead), or 'none' (write no header and remove a copy an earlier run
left, so the build's include path supplies it). The default keeps the
always-refreshed template that the comment argues for.

Tests: test_runtime_header_option.py covers the three forms, including
that 'none' removes a stale copy and leaves the rest of the output.
---
 tools/recomp/__main__.py                   |  9 +++
 tools/recomp/test_runtime_header_option.py | 64 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 tools/recomp/translator.py                 | 56 +++++++++++++------
 3 files changed, 113 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 tools/recomp/test_runtime_header_option.py

